<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Fun Outdoors! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love to Run!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Speaking: Productive: Express basic needs and feelings using simple phrases.<br>Interactive: Interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answers about familiar things using simple phrases.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Speaking: Can describe personal preferences and feelings for activities using simple sentences (e.g., "I love to swim.").<br>Can ask and answer predictable questions about preferences (e.g., "Do you like to ride bikes?" "Yes, I like it.").<br>Can express basic needs to peers using simple phrases related to outdoor activities (e.g., "You need to run." or "You need to jump.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Let's Move Outdoors!" TPR activity. The teacher calls out an activity, and students perform the action. "Let's run fast!" (students run in place quickly), "Let's jump high!" (students jump), "Let's swim slowly!" (students do slow swimming motions). This activates prior knowledge of verbs and adverbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of outdoor activities (run, jump, swim, play soccer, ride bike) and models target phrases, emphasizing clear pronunciation. "I love to run!" (Teacher points to self and flashcard, exaggerates /r/ sound). "I like to swim!" (Teacher points to self and flashcard, exaggerates /s/ sound). "I play soccer. It's fun!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions to check understanding.<br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1 (showing "run" flashcard): "Is this 'run' or 'swim'?" (Students say "Run!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2 (showing "swim" flashcard): "Do I swim in the park or in the water?" (Students point to water or say "Water!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Do you like to run?" and models "Yes, I like to run!" for students to repeat chorally.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or on large flashcards. The dialogue features two friends, Leo and Mia, discussing their favorite activities at the park. Example:
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "Hi Mia! Do you like to play at the park?"
                <br><b>Mia:</b> "Yes, I love to play! I love to run fast!"
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "Oh, I like to jump high! It's fun. Do you like to swim?"
                <br><b>Mia:</b> "No, I don't like to swim. I like to ride my bike slowly."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing preferences for outdoor activities and asking questions about others' preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Oral):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud clearly, pointing to the characters. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Who likes to run fast?" (Students point to Mia or say "Mia!"). "Who likes to jump high?" (Students point to Leo or say "Leo!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Oral):</b> Teacher says: "Mia likes to swim." (Students say "False!"). Teacher says: "Leo likes to jump." (Students say "True!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice expressing preferences and asking questions using "Subject + verb + adverb" (e.g., "I run fast."), Present Simple ("I love to run."), and WH-questions ("Do you like...?"). Target vocabulary: run, jump, swim, play, like, love, fast, slow, fun.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Choral Repetition and Fill-in-the-Blank (Oral):</b> The teacher shows flashcards of outdoor activities and reads sentence frames. Students complete chorally.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "I love to ____." (shows 'run' flashcard). Students: "I love to run!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "I ____ fast." (shows 'run' flashcard). Students: "I run fast!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "I like to ____." (shows 'swim' flashcard). Students: "I like to swim!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "It's ____." (shows 'fun' picture). Students: "It's fun!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Focus on /r/ and /s/ pronunciation during repetition.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Picture Prompt Sentences (Oral):</b> The teacher displays a sequence of different action flashcards (e.g., 'play soccer', 'ride bike', 'climb') and adjectives/adverbs (e.g., 'fun', 'happy', 'quickly'). Students create simple sentences using "I like/love to [verb]. It's [adjective/adverb]." or "I [verb] [adverb]." individually or in small groups.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows "play soccer" + "fun." Student 1: "I love to play soccer. It's fun!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows "ride bike" + "slowly." Student 2: "I ride my bike slowly."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction – Q&A Chain:</b> Students receive a set of mini-flashcards for outdoor activities. In pairs, Student A asks, "Do you like to [activity on card]?" Student B responds, "Yes, I like it!" or "No, I don't like it." Then Student B asks Student A a different question. They switch roles and continue. Encourage adding "It's fun!" or "It's fast!" to their answers.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Outdoor Activity Buddy Interview" – A collaborative pair interview where students ask and answer about their preferences for outdoor activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will use target language to discuss what they love to do in a "Fun Outdoors!" setting, connecting to the theme "I Love to Run!" by expressing their preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students are paired up. Each pair receives a small sheet with pictures of various outdoor activities (run, jump, swim, play soccer, ride bike, climb).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Student A starts by picking one activity picture and asking Student B: "Do you like to [activity]?" (e.g., "Do you like to run?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Student B answers using complete sentences: "Yes, I love to [activity]! It's fun/fast/slow." or "No, I don't like to [activity]." (e.g., "Yes, I love to run! It's fast!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Student B then asks Student A about a different activity. They continue interviewing each other about all the activities on their sheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Encourage students to also suggest activities to their friend using "You need to [activity]!" (e.g., "You need to jump!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">6. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting for natural interaction, focusing on fluency and communication.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and student self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Outdoor Activity Buddy Interview" focusing on individual student participation and language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Outdoor Activity Buddy Interview," demonstrating their ability to ask and answer questions about preferences for outdoor activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key sounds, especially /r/ in "run" and /s/ in "swim."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., run, jump, swim, like, love, fast, fun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask "Do you like to [activity]?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer "Yes, I like it." / "No, I don't." or "I love to [activity]! It's [adjective/adverb]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Express basic needs/suggestions using "You need to [activity]."</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can say: 'I love to _____'." (Students complete orally).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask my friend: 'Do you like to _____?'" (Students complete orally).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review and connection to next lesson)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What outdoor activity do you love to do with your friends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new action word did you say today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., TPR, pair work for generating speech, visual aids, pronunciation drills).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in expressing preferences or asking questions? (e.g., correct verb usage, forming full sentences, specific adverbs). How can I scaffold for these in future lessons? (e.g., more sentence frames, repetition, specific sound practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the speaking outcomes met?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about outdoor activities and preferences. The next lesson (Lesson 4) will build on these oral expressions, moving into writing simple sentences about personal preferences for outdoor activities, using the same vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>